hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to  another edition of ask Evan in today's  episode I'm going to answer a question  from tomorrow's one of my newsletter  readers who wants to put on a music  festival as his business and it's had a  hard time raising capital for to get the  sponsors to help them launch it and one  of my advice on how to how to get that  thing going so a couple things if you  are going after sponsorship they want to  see that you have something it's really  hard to go out their businesses to say  hey I'd like you to sponsor me and give  me whatever it is five hundred bucks ten  thousand dollars whatever it is that you  need from them when there's nothing for  them to look at beyond just an idea so  you want to build up momentum so what I  would do with you you're starting up its  movement around youth especially with a  with a music concert is kick up a lot of  noise in social media so build a Twitter  following build a facebook following  talk with what you want to do get people  excited about it get people come into  your site and interacting you want to  build up as much momentum as you can as  possible to get it going start a  newsletter create a YouTube channel if  you need to try to get people organized  in some way so if you have a flash mob  or something to get on the media's  attention as well just to start building  some momentum so you're not an unknown  quantity if you're not going to a  business say hey I have an idea it's  just on paper and there's nothing to  back you up you have also mentum if you  can see you have thousands of people  following you on facebook and twitter  and on your newsletter that you can  access you know right away that's an  opportunity that businesses would jump  on so first take that time to build up a  following build up an audience through  social media through your newsletter etc  the next thing I would do is when you're  going after your first sponsors try to  go for in-kind partnerships and that's a  lot easier to get in kind donations  instead of money up front so just depend  on what you need for your for your event  I'm sure there's a whole bunch of stuff  that you'll need that you would use the  money to go and buy you might be able to  get those guys to be able to give you  that amount of dollars but not in money  but in actual products or services so  think about all the products and  services you need to spend to get this  event going talk to those companies and  see if they'd be willing to donate some  of their time or some other products or  prize  is to be able to you know promote  themselves out the event it's a lot easy  for a company to give up some of their  inventory or to give up some of their  service time that they may have extra  time anyway with their company instead  of giving you cash right and that can be  your lead-in nobody needs to know that  they're they're only given you you know  in-kind donations but you can start  raising a lot of you know dollar value  items for the event so you can say you  know you've raised $50,000 or whatever  it is because you have you know in-kind  products or services so that's kind of  step one then you can use that to go  after other businesses you can say well  we've already partnered up with these  companies and we've already got all this  attention happening on social media and  on our newsletter and some you know many  many events that you've done that starts  building the momentum so now when you go  in you're talking to companies you have  a lot behind you and it feels like  they're going to be missing out if they  don't join on the boat it's a lot better  than just going in with a piece of paper  and saying here's an idea we have but we  don't have any traction so that's what I  encourage you to do and this applies for  anything your business you know when  companies pitched us about raising  capital before if they just came in with  an idea they usually got to know but  they came in with some momentum  